>> Interface is not gameplay.
>
> Actually, it can be; Crawl's auto-item-search for things you don't
> remember where they are is actually a gameplay change disguised as
> interface assistance.

I'll agree with this, at least by the logic that it makes something easy
that was previously possible but not worth doing.

> Why?  It removes the need for the player to bring potentially-useful
> items with him to a known location; if it isn't something he needs right
> then or in the near future, he can leave it on the ground and come back
> for it later with autosearch.  This in turn removes a lot of weight
> encumbrance issues from the player since he's not lugging around a bunch
> of stuff from place to place; this in TURN means monsters don't jump out
> at your player while he's Burdened, say, forcing him to make quick
> decisions about what to do.

Well, Crawl players still stash wands, scrolls, and potions like NetHack
players do, for the most part. You can't just leave stuff on the floor
everywhere or monsters might pick it up and use it. What Crawl players
don't do is worry about armor and weapons until they need to. (and to a 
lesser extent, tool-like items)

When playing NetHack, I know that if I ever want to see an item again,
I need to drop it off next to a stairwell I intend to use again, or at
the stash spot I am visiting regularly. Otherwise, I will lose track
of it.  In times of desperation I have just grepped my ttyrecs to find
items, because checking every square of the damn dungeon for the Bell
of Opening just _isn't fun_. I can hear a couple of you snickering now:
"Then don't drop the Bell!" What if it was a typo? "Then don't make
typographical errors!" Yeah, uh, sure :)

Crawl players still have burdening issues, and inventory management
issues; the amount you can carry is generally much _lower_, unless you
are a high-STR character, and you only have 52 inventory slots no matter
what you do. So, yes, as you say below, there are gameplay effects,
and if NetHack had automatic stash tracking, probably it would need some
other gameplay changes. I don't think it would end Burdening as a problem,
though, it would just reduce constantly carrying crap to the stash site,
at least for me. (Because I don't know what I am going to need until later
in the game, I save everything I might need; that's a lot of things.)

> Whether this is a good or bad thing is certainly up for debate, and I'm
> not personally sure where I stand on the above... but interface changes
> can, indeed, have a gameplay effect if the designer isn't careful.

Oh absolutely. They can also have a "how fun is the game to play" effect
though, and I think that, even though it would take extra work, NetHack 
could benefit from a number of interface enhancements, including stash 
tracking and better (interlevel, especially!) autotravel.

